  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "Consider the equation $\\sin\\left(x+\\frac\\pi4\\right)=2\\cos\\left(x-\\frac\\pi4\\right)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use compound-angle formulae to show that $\\tan x=-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-\\sin x=\\cos x$, hence $\\tan x=-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\sin x\\cos\\frac\\pi4+\\cos x\\sin\\frac\\pi4&=2\\left(\\cos x\\cos\\frac\\pi4+\\sin x\\sin\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\\\\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\sin x+\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\cos x&=\\frac2{\\sqrt2}\\cos x+\\frac2{\\sqrt2}\\sin x\\\\\\sin x+\\cos x&=2\\cos x+2\\sin x\\\\-\\sin x&=\\cos x\\\\\\tan x&=-1\\end{aligned}$$\nHere $\\cos x\\ne0$: otherwise the preceding equation would also require $\\sin x=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence solve the equation for $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac{3\\pi}4,\\frac{7\\pi}4$.",
          "worked": "The reference angle is $\\frac\\pi4$, and tangent is negative in quadrants II and IV. Thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi}4$ or $\\frac{7\\pi}4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      }
    ]
  },
